 Serverless computing is a cloud computing execution model where the cloud provider dynamically manages the allocation of machine resources. In this model, developers write and deploy code without having to provision or manage servers. Instead, the cloud provider automatically executes the code in response to events, such as HTTP requests or changes to a database.

In serverless computing, the cloud provider charges for the actual execution time of the code, rather than for the provisioned server capacity. This can lead to cost savings, as you only pay for the resources you use. However, it's important to note that serverless computing may have higher operational complexity due to the need to manage event triggers, API gateways, and other components.

Here's an example of a simple serverless function written in Python using AWS Lambda:

```python
def add(a, b):
    return a + b
```

This function can be triggered by an HTTP request through AWS API Gateway, and it will perform addition on the provided numbers `a` and `b`. The actual execution of the function will be managed by AWS Lambda.

In terms of infrastructure as code, you can use Terraform to deploy and manage the AWS resources required for this serverless function:

```hcl
provider "aws" {
  region = "us-west-2"
}

resource "aws_lambda_function" "add" {
  filename      = "add.py"
  function_name = "add"
  handler       = "add.add"
  runtime       = "python3.8"
  role          = aws_iam_role.lambda_basic_execution.arn

  environment {
    variables = {
      key1 = "value1"
      key2 = "value2"
    }
  }
}

resource "aws_iam_role" "lambda_basic_execution" {
  name = "lambda_basic_execution"

  assume_role_policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17"
    Statement = [
      {
        Action = "sts:AssumeRole"
        Effect = "Allow"
        Principal = {
          Service = "lambda.amazonaws.com"
        }
      }
    ]
  })
}

resource "aws_api_gateway_rest_api" "api" {
  name        = "api"
  description = "API for serverless function"
}

resource "aws_api_gateway_resource" "resource" {
  rest_api_id = aws_api_gateway_rest_api.api.id
  parent_id   = aws_api_gateway_rest_api.api.root_resource_id
  path_part   = "add"
}

resource "aws_api_gateway_method" "method" {
  rest_api_id   = aws_api_gateway_rest_api.api.id
  resource_id   = aws_api_gateway_resource.resource.id
  http_method   = "ANY"
  authorization = "NONE"
}

resource "aws_api_gateway_integration" "integration" {
  rest_api_id             = aws_api_gateway_rest_api.api.id
  resource_id             = aws_api_gateway_resource.resource.id
  http_method             = aws_api_gateway_method.method.http_method
  integration_http_method = "POST"
  type                    = "AWS_PROXY"
  uri                     = aws_lambda_function.add.invoke_arn
}

resource "aws_api_gateway_deployment" "deployment" {
  rest_api_id = aws_api_gateway_rest_api.api.id
  stage_name  = "prod"
}
```

This Terraform configuration creates an AWS Lambda function, an API Gateway, and sets up the necessary integration between the two to allow HTTP requests to trigger the serverless function.

